## INTRODUCTION
Metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline porous materials with regular cavities and channels of molecular dimensions that have demonstrated many applications in gas capture and storage, separation, sensing, and catalysis.1−7 Investigations of the structure−property relationships in MOFs are primarily based on the average, periodic (crystallographic) structures determined by single-crystal or powder X-ray diffraction.8 It is generally recognized that local, nonperiodic structural features such as crystal surfaces and interfaces, guest molecules, and point- or extended-defects, have a major impact on MOF properties, and often determine their performance in many applications.9−12 The structure of the crystal surface, for instance, determines the accessibility of the adsorptive or catalytically active sites inside the crystals and the way the crystals assemble.13−15 Unfortunately, the direct probing of local structures in MOFs remains a challenging task, due to the lack of suitable characterization tools.
Recently, we developed a novel low-dose high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) method that enables the direct imaging of electron beam-sensitive materials without damaging their intrinsic structures.16,17 This method has opened new avenues toward real-space observation of fragile crystalline structures, including various MOFs, at the atomic level. Using this method, we have successfully observed the interfacial structure between the intergrown ZIF-8 crystals,16 the guest molecules encapsulated in NU-1000,18 as well as the “missing-linker” and “missing cluster” defects in UiO-66.19
$\mathrm{MIL-}101\mathrm{(Cr)}$ is one of the most widely studied MOFs,20−23 due to its wide-open framework, exceptionally large surface area, and excellent chemical stability; all these characteristics are particularly advantageous for catalytic applications.24−33 The synthesis of MOFs is generally performed using various inorganic and organic acid additives that can modulate the crystallization process to achieve better control in crystal size or to increase the product yield. MIL-101(Cr) is usually synthesized using hydrofluoric acid (HF) as additive, but other acids such as acetic acid $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH})$ and hydrochloric acid (HCl) have also been used.21,29,34,35 Previous studies reported that different additives could lead to different crystal sizes, surface areas, and product yields.36 However, the effect of additives on the crystal structure of MIL-101(Cr) remains unknown.
Here, we use the novel low-dose HRTEM technique to probe subtle differences in the surface structure between MOF MIL-101(Cr) samples synthesized with different additives. We also find that a vacuum heating treatment can modify the surface structure of MIL-101(Cr), and that the required temperature to achieve this modification depends on the original synthetic conditions. These observations imply that the $\mathrm{MIL\mathrm{-}101\big(C r\big)}$ samples we have investigated differ in thermal stability, which we verify using in situ heating powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) experiments.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The structure of $\mathbf{MIL-}101{\bigl(}\mathbf{Cr}{\bigr)}$ is built from the linkage of benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate (BDC) ligands and chromium clusters; it has a giant cubic unit cell ${\mathit{\check{a}}}\approx9\ {\mathrm{\nm}}$ ; space group $F d\mathbf{-}3m)$ with the nominal chemical formula $\big[\mathrm{Cr}_{3}(\mathrm{O})$ - $\mathrm{{X}(B D C)_{3}(H_{2}O)_{2}]}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaX=F}$ or OH).26 Figure 1a illustrates the structure: four $\mu_{3}\mathrm{O}$ -bridged trimeric chromium clusters are linked by six BDCs to form a hybrid supertetrahedron (ST); the STs are further assembled via corner-sharing in a similar way that the tetrahedral $\mathrm{TO}_{4}$ ( $\mathrm{\dot{T}=S i}$ or Al) building units of inorganic zeolites are assembled into the MTN-type framework. The MTN zeotype architecture of MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ consists of two types of mesoporous cages: medium cages built on 20
STs ( ${\sim}2.9~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter), and large cages built on 28 STs (∼3.4 nm in diameter).26
The projected structure of MIL-101(Cr), along the ⟨110⟩ direction, can be viewed as a layer-by-layer stacking of medium cages that are aligned along this projection (Figure 1a). MIL$10\mathrm{{i}(C r)}$ crystals typically exhibit an octahedral morphology, exposing eight $\{111\}$ surfaces (Figure 1b). When the octahedral crystal is oriented along the ⟨110⟩ direction, four $\{111\}$ surfaces are in the edge-on configuration (Figure 1c), allowing their structures to be directly observed by HRTEM. Unlike in the bulk structure, for which each medium cage is surrounded by 10 STs (in this projection), there might be incomplete cages on the crystal surface of $\mathbf{MIL-}10\mathbf{\bar{l}}(\mathbf{Cr})$ . Therefore, we use the “completeness” of the medium cages at the outermost layer, which is defined as the percentage of the residual STs, to describe the surface structure. Figure 1d shows the structural models for three representative states: $100\%$ , $80\%$ , and $60\%$ “completeness”, with 10, 8, and 6 residual STs, respectively. A smaller value of “completeness” corresponds to a more open cage.
In our study, we synthesize three different MIL-101(Cr) samples, using either HF (denoted as MIL-101-HF), acetic acid (MIL-101-Ac), or no additive at all (MIL-101-NA); all samples are prepared using the same synthetic procedure, except for the use of an equimolar (relative to chromium and BDC) amount of additive in the first two samples (HF and acetic acid) (see Experimental Section for the detailed synthetic procedure). We find that all three samples exhibit an octahedral crystal shape, but differ in crystal size: MIL-101- HF $\mathrm{\left(700-800~nm\right)~>~MIL\mathrm{-}101\mathrm{-}N A}$ $\left(350{-}450\ \mathrm{nm}\right)\ >\ \mathrm{MIL}.$ 101-Ac $\mathrm{^{\prime}}150{-}250\ \mathrm{nm},$ ). All samples show characteristic PXRD patterns and $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of the MIL-101 structure (see Figure S1). MIL-101-Ac has a lower BET surface area, compared to the other two samples, which can be attributed to the larger proportion of external surface in this material associated with its smaller crystal sizes.
To examine the crystal structures of the samples in greater detail, we performed additional investigations using our lowdose HRTEM method. Note that the HRTEM images presented in Figures 2−4 were acquired at an extremely low dose of electrons $(\sim8~\mathrm{~e~}^{-}\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-2})$ , and processed to be more directly interpretable by correcting the effect of the contrast transfer function (CTF) of the objective lens. Raw images can be found in the Supporting Information (Figures S2 and S3). We first investigate the bulk structure with HRTEM to find out how much structural information (image resolution) we can achieve and assess whether or not the low-dose imaging conditions used in our experiments are sufficiently safe to preserve the original crystal structure. Figure 2a shows a typical HRTEM image of MIL-101-NA that was acquired at one vertex of the octahedral crystal along the ⟨110⟩ zone axis. A highly ordered stacking of medium cages is observed in this projection, which is consistent with the crystal structure of MIL-101 solved from PXRD. A fast Fourier transform (FFT) of this image shows a transfer of information as high as $2.5\textup{\AA}$ (Figure 2a, inset). This high resolution is a testament to the intact structure of MIL-101(Cr) under our imaging conditions, and also suggests that great structural details can be explored from the image. Indeed, despite the very crowded arrangement of atoms in this structure $>5000$ atoms in a single unit cell), the main structural features, including the medium cages and the connections of the surrounding STs that form triangular and pentagonal patterns between the medium cages, can be identified very clearly from the image contrast of the enlarged image (Figure 2b); it shows a perfect match between the projected structural model and the simulated electrostatic potential map (Figure 2c). We also perform HRTEM simulation, using the actual imaging conditions, to compare with the experimental raw image; a perfect match between the simulated and experimental images is observed, when the specimen thickness is set to be $\sim60~\mathrm{nm}$ (see SI Figure S2).
Similar HRTEM images of the bulk structure of the other two MIL-101 $\langle\mathrm{Cr}\rangle$ samples confirm that the dose of electrons used for imaging is sufficiently low to avoid any structural damage and preserve the integrity of the original structure. The high resolution of the images allows resolving individual trimeric $\mathrm{Cr}$ clusters, but the BDC linkers cannot be clearly identified, due to the extreme structural complexity and the low contrast of organic species (Figures 2c). Therefore, the following descriptions of the crystal surface structure are based on the observed positions of Cr clusters, whereas the presented surface models with terminating ligands are surmised from the results of theoretical calculations (see the Computational section in the Supporting Information).
Although the three MIL-101 $\mathrm{(cr)}$ samples have the same bulk structure, their surface structures are different. Figure $3a-$ c shows the ⟨110⟩ HRTEM images of three freshly synthesized samples that are crystallized at $210~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8{\mathrm{~h~}}$ , then washed with DMF followed by methanol, and dried at room temperature. These images clearly show that MIL-101-HF and MIL-101-NA have uniform $\{111\}$ surfaces that terminated by nearly, but not completely, closed medium cages. The “completeness” of the surface cages of these two samples is $80\%$ , according to the image contrast. In the case of MIL-101- Ac, however, the {111} surface consists uniformly of medium cages with $100\%$ “completeness”. These results reveal, for the first time, that acid additives play a significant role in determining the crystal surface structure of MOFs.
It is worth noting that the surface structure can be further modified by a postsynthesis treatment. As shown in Figure 3d, after being heated at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum $(\sim5~\mathrm{{mbar})}$ for 24 h, MIL-101-HF exhibits an altered surface structure, exposing medium cages with $60\%$ “completeness”. This indicates that, during the thermal treatment, the cages at the crystal surface of MIL-101-HF are being open, even though the bulk structure is unchanged, according to PXRD and HRTEM observations. Interestingly, the same treatment does not result in an opening of the surface cages in MIL-101-NA or MIL-101-Ac, and their original surface structures are retained (Figure 3e and f). Hence, we totally identify three types of surface structure in these MIL-101 $\left(\mathrm{Cr}\right)$ samples, with surface cages at different degrees of completeness: $60\%$ , $80\%$ , and $100\%$ (Figure 3d−f). These surface structures are confirmed using a separate electron microscopy technique, integrated differential phase contrast scanning transmission electron microscopy (iDPCSTEM), which gives a slightly lower image resolution, but a stronger image contrast, in comparison with HRTEM (Figure $3\mathrm{g-i)}$ .
It has been realized that the surface structure is more prone to beam damage than the bulk structure.37 In our study, we rule out the possibility that the observed surface structures are results of beam-induced damage from two aspects. First, we determine that the bulk structure of MIL-101 can afford ${\sim}16$ $\mathrm{e}^{-}/\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ before damage occurs (see Figure S4); we therefore use a remarkably lower electron dose, $8~\mathrm{{e}}^{-}/\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ , to image the surface structures. Second, we find the correlation between the surface structures and the synthesis/treatment conditions to be highly reproducible, as we repeat the synthesis and imaging at least three times for each sample. This reproducibility is a testament to that the observed surface structures are intrinsic, because beam-induced structural change, if there are any, should occur in a random instead of in such a consistent manner.
We also synthesized MIL-101(Cr) using sodium fluoride (NaF) and hydrochloric acid (HCl) as additives and investigated them with HRTEM. The sample obtained with NaF is similar to MIL-101-HF with respect to its surface structure and response to a vacuum heating treatment at 150 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ the sample obtained with HCl has a similar surface structure as MIL-101-Ac, but its surface cages become open upon the vacuum heating at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (see Table 1 and Figure S5).
aThe vacuum heating treatment is conducted at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ ; the surface structure is described by the “completeness” of the mesoporous cages at the outermost layer.
Further investigations show that MIL-101-NA and MIL101-Ac can undergo a similar change of surface structure (i.e., cage opening) as the one observed for MIL-101-HF, after a vacuum heating treatment at the higher temperature of $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (see Figure S6). This suggests that MIL-101(Cr) samples synthesized with different additives have different thermal stabilities under vacuum. We speculate that, if such differences exist, they should not be solely related to the local surface structure (observed by HRTEM), but also associated with the bulk structure. Therefore, we conduct in situ PXRD to monitor the structural degradations of MIL-101-HF and MIL-101-Ac. MIL-101-Ac consists of crystals that are much smaller $\sim50\AA$ fold in volume) than those in MIL-101-HF (Figure S1). It is conceivable that smaller crystals are more prone to structural degradation than larger crystals, assuming identical framework properties. Interestingly, the in situ PXRD measurements showed the counterintuitive result that MIL-101-Ac is more stable than MIL-101-HF upon vacuum heating, which is actually consistent with the HRTEM observations of surface structures. As shown in Figure 4, the intensity of diffraction peaks associated with the MIL-101 structure gradually decreases as the temperature increases, for both materials; however, a significant difference is clearly observed at 275−285 ${}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ at which the relevant peaks are largely retained in MIL101-Ac but almost vanish in MIL-101-HF; MIL-101-Ac loses its crystallinity completely for temperatures ${>}300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Taken together, these results demonstrate that MIL-101-Ac is slightly more resistant to vacuum heating than MIL-101-HF. The only difference in the framework between MIL-101-HF and MIL101-Ac is that it is $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ in the former, but $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ in the latter, that balances the positive charge of the chromium cluster. We therefore conclude that the counterions bonded to the chromium clusters have an impact on the stability of the framework.
We attempt to understand this impact through theoretical calculations by simplifying the degradation of the MIL$101\big(\mathrm{Cr}\big)$ framework as a hydrolysis reaction of an isolate $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{HBDC}\right)_{6}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{2}\mathrm{F}/\mathrm{OH}$ cluster that leads to the release of a $\mathrm{\bf~H}_{2}\mathrm{\bfBDC}$ ligand from the cluster, because it is difficult to model the entire system due to the giant unit cell of MIL-101 with a high level of charge-compensating anion disorder. This simplification is based on the fact that MIL-101 contains a considerable amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as guest molecules after heating at $150\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C},^{20}$ and a reasonable assumption that the structural degradation takes place at the coordination bond between the BDC carboxyl group and Cr. As illustrated in Figure 5, the reaction consists of three elementary steps: (i) an adsorbed $_\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule bonds to a $\mathrm{Cr}$ site by replacing the $_\mathrm{C-O}$ group of BDC; (ii) a second $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecule interacts with another $\mathrm{Cr}$ at the same cluster, and (iii) a proton is transferred from $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ to the dangling BDC, leading to its complete detachment from the cluster and a residual $-\mathrm{OH}$ on the cluster.
We investigate the thermodynamics of this hydrolysis process by calculating the free energy change $(\Delta G_{\mathrm{React}})$ of each step aforementioned. In the case of $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}$ - $(\mathrm{HBDC})_{6}\mathrm{\bar{(}H_{2}O)}_{2}\mathrm{F},$ the calculated $\Delta G_{\mathrm{React}}$ of the three sequential steps are -1.48, 16.78, and $-39.41\mathrm{~\kJ/mol},$ respectively, at $575\ \mathrm{K},$ and 3.81, 9.70, and - $\cdot19.85\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ at $425~\mathrm{K};$ in the case of $\mathrm{Cr}_{3}\mathrm{O}\left(\mathrm{HBDC}\right)_{6}\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)_{2}\mathrm{OH},$ the $\Delta G_{\mathrm{React}}$ values are 27.62, 30.32, and $-111.15\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ at $575\ \mathrm{K},$ and 24.02, 17.58, and $-64.59\mathrm{~\kJ/mol}$ at 425 K. These results indicate that the first two steps $\mathrm{~\cal~H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ insertion) on the $-\mathrm{OH}$ compensated cluster are more endothermic in comparison with those on the $-\mathrm{F}$ compensated cluster, whereas the last step (proton transfer) has an opposite trend. The different reactivity of $-\mathrm{F}$ and $-\mathrm{OH}$ compensated clusters can be attributed to their different affinity to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ that determines the endothermicity of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ insertion and proton transfer steps. This provides a rational explanation for the observed slight difference in stability between MIL-101-HF and MIL-101-Ac. The in situ PXRD of MIL-101-HF reveals another interesting phenomenon, as shown in Figure 4a. In this figure, we observe a set of diffraction peaks emerging at ${\sim}150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and further evolving as the temperature increases, in parallel with the degradation of the peaks related to the MIL-101 structure. This new set of diffraction peaks becomes the most intense at $285^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and disappears at $325^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Figure 4a). We find that this set of peaks can be indexed to another type of $\mathrm{Cr}$ -BDC MOF, ${\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}53{\mathrm{(Cr)}}$ , which is known to be thermally more stable, compared to MIL-101.38 There are two possibilities that account for this phenomenon: (i) a phase transformation from MIL-101 to MIL-53 that takes place during the vacuum heating treatment; (ii) the presence of MIL-53 crystals in MIL101-HF as impurities that undergo recrystallization (ripening) and become larger and more noticeable during vacuum heating, when the MIL-101 structure degrades. A careful HRTEM examination reveals that, in freshly synthesized MIL101-HF, there is indeed a small number of rod-shaped crystals that exhibit the MIL-53 structure, in addition to the major octahedral MIL-101 crystals (Figure 6a−c). We note that MIL
$53(\mathrm{Cr})$ has a flexible framework with varied unit cell parameters in different systems ,39−41 and that the MIL-53 structure observed here exhibits an orthorhombic unit cell $\overset{\cdot}{\underset{\cdot}{a}}=$ 10.3(4) Å; $b=19.03(5)$ Å; $c=8.4(2)\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ ), as determined from PXRD and confirmed by HRTEM (see Figure S7). After vacuum heating at ${285~^{\circ}C}_{\mathrm{{\scriptscriptstylei}}}$ , the octahedral crystals become much smaller in size and lose their crystallinity, while the rodlike crystals increase in size and number and retain a high crystallinity (Figure 6d−f). These observations indicate that the degradation of MIL-101 structure coincides with a severe shrinkage MIL-101 crystals and an evolvement of MIL-53 crystals, which is consistent with the in situ XRD results. Given the fact that the vacuum heating treatment does not involve any solvent, the phase transformation from MIL-101 to MIL53 should be a solid−solid one. The MIL-53 impurity present in MIL-101-HF might play an important role in inducing the phase transformation, for example, by serving as seeds. We do not observe any MIL-53 impurity in MIL-101-NA and MIL101-Ac, which are synthesized under exactly the same conditions as MIL-101-HF, except for the additives.
It is worth noting that the difference in thermal stability between different MIL-101(Cr) samples is only observed under vacuum conditions. When these samples are heated in ${\bf N}_{2},$ at ambient pressure, they exhibited similar stability up to $325^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and the crystal growth of MIL-53 impurity in MIL101-HF was not observed (Figure S8). These results imply that applying vacuum may facilitate structural changes (e.g., degradation, phase transformation, and ripening) of MOF crystals.
## CONCLUSIONS
In this work, we use a novel, low-dose HRTEM technique to probe both the bulk and the local structures of MIL-101 $(\mathrm{Cr})$ , at nearly atomic resolution. This technique allows us to discover, for the first time, that the surface structure of MIL
$101{\big(}\mathrm{Cr}{\big)}$ can be tuned by using different additives during the synthesis, and by a postsynthesis thermal treatment. We identify three typical surface structures, each one exhibiting a different degree of “completeness” (or an opening degree) of the surface mesoporous cages. We show that a thermal treatment performed under vacuum, at specific temperatures, can selectively open up the surface cages without changing the bulk structure, and that the required temperature is dependent on the type of additive used during the synthesis. These observations, in combination with in situ XRD measurements, demonstrate that the additives have a significant influence not only on the size of the crystal, but also on the surface structure and the stability of MIL-101(Cr)’s framework. Solid−solid phase transformation from MIL-101 to MIL-53 takes place only when HF is used for the synthesis and vacuum is applied for the thermal treatment. The chemistry behind these interesting phenomena is still unclear and it will require more investigations to fully understand their underlying mechanisms.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Synthesis. The synthetic procedure of MIL-101-NA is as follows: $0.664\ \mathrm{g}$ of terephthalic acid $\left(4\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ and $1.6\ \mathrm{g}$ of $\mathrm{Cr}\big(\mathrm{NO}_{3}\big)_{3}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (4 mmol) were added into $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of distilled water. The suspension was magnetically stirred for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ at room temperature and then transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave with a capacity of $45~\mathrm{mL}$ . The autoclave was heated to $210~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a heating rate of $1.5~\mathrm{{^\circC/min}}$ and then maintained at $210^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for another $^{8\mathrm{{h}}}$ . The obtained solid product was collected by centrifugation $(8500\ \mathrm{rpm},\ 10\ \mathrm{min})$ , washed with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ three times, and further purified using a three-step procedure: (i) soaked in DMF for $30\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature, during which the soaking solvent was replaced by fresh DMF every $^{10\mathrm{h};}$ (ii) soaked in methanol for $30\mathrm{~h~}$ and replaced by fresh methanol every $10\mathrm{~h~}$ ; (iii) dried under vacuum $(\sim5\ \mathrm{mbar})$ at room temperature for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ .
The other MIL-101(Cr) samples were synthesized and purified using the same procedure as described above except that 4 mmol of the chosen additive (HF, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COOH}$ , HCl or NaF) was added into the initial reaction suspension.
The as-synthesized samples are denoted as “freshly synthesized”. Some selected samples that were further treated under vacuum $(\sim5$ mbar) at an elevated temperature (150 or $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) for another $24\ \mathrm{h},$ are denoted as “vacuum heated”.
Characterizations. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were recorded in the $2\theta$ range from $2^{\circ}$ to $20^{\circ}$ with a step of $0.01^{\circ}$ on a Bruker AXS D8 advance diffractometer using $\operatorname{Cu}\operatorname{K}\alpha$ radiation $\langle\lambda=$ 1.5418 Å). The working voltage and current were $40~\mathrm{kV}$ and $40\ \mathrm{mA},$ respectively. The in situ heating PXRD experiments were performed on a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer equipped with a high temperature oven chamber (Anton Paar HTK 1200N) at a vacuum of ${\sim}10^{\overline{{-3}}}$ mbar. The heating process started from room temperature with a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ and stay for $3\ \mathrm{min}$ at each measured temperature. The in situ heating PXRD patterns were collected in the $2\theta$ range from $4.5^{\circ}$ to $20^{\circ}$ with a step of $0.02^{\circ}$ . Nitrogen adsorption/ desorption isotherms were collected at $77~\mathrm{K}$ using a Micromeritics 3Flex system. Before measurements, all the samples were degassed at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The surface areas were determined by the Brunauer− Emmett−Teller (BET) method. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was performed using a FEI Nova 450 SEM microscope operated at $3~\mathrm{kV}$ . The SEM specimen was coated with a thin layer of Au $(\sim3\ \mathrm{nm})$ by sputter deposition to reduce the charging effect.
Low-dose high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) was performed on a Cs-corrected FEI Titan electron microscope operated at $300\mathrm{kV}.$ . Spherical aberration was corrected in a range of $\pm2\ \mu\mathrm{m}$ . Specimen searching, zone axis alignment, and prefocusing were conducted at $13000\times$ magnification. HRTEM images were acquired at $44000\times$ magnification by using a Gatan K2 direct-detection camera operated in the electron counting mode with the dose fractionation function. Each image stack is composed of 120 frames with 0.05 s exposure for each frame, corresponding to a total exposure of 6 s and a total electron dose of ${\sim}8~\bar{\mathrm{e}}/\bar{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ . The detailed HRTEM image acquisition and processing (zone axis alignment, focus determination, and CTF-correction) methods have been presented in ref 17.
Integrated differential phase contrast scanning transmission electron microscopy (iDPC-STEM) was performed on a double Cscorrected electron microscope (FEI Titan Cubed Themis Z) operated at $300~\mathrm{kV}.$ . The iDPC-STEM images were acquired using the fourquadrant DF4 detector, with a convergence semiangle of 14.9 mrad and collection angles of $16{-}61$ mrad. The employed probe current was $\mathrm{\sim}1\ \mathrm{pA},$ with a total exposure time of $12.6\ s$ . The total electron dose for each image was ${\sim}\hat{3}\hat{5}~\mathrm{e}/\hat{\mathrm{A}}^{2}$ . A high-pass filter was applied to reduce the low-frequency noises.
Theoretical Calculations. Detailed calculation methods are provided in the computational section in the Supporting Information.
